WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
See, we draw a few pictures of neural networks.

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:05.960
In this video, we'll talk about exactly what those pictures means.

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:11.360
In other words, exactly what those little neural networks we've been drawing represent.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:16.160
And we'll start with focusing on the case of neural networks with what's called a single

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:17.160
hidden layer.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.000
Here's a picture of a neural net.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.120
Let's give different parts of these pictures some names.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:28.200
We have the input features, x1, x2, x3, stacked up vertically, and this is called

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.560
the input layer of the neural network.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.160
So maybe not surprisingly, this contains the inputs to the neural network.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:41.640
Then there's another layer of circles, and this is called a hidden layer of the neural

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:42.640
network.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:45.480
I'll come back in a second to say what the word hidden means.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:50.320
But the final layer here is formed by, in this case, just one node, and this single

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:55.800
node layer is called the output layer, and it's responsible for generating the predicted

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.280
value y hat.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.160
In a neural network that you train with supervised learning, the training set contains values

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.520
of the inputs x as well as the target outputs y.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:10.720
So the term hidden layer refers to the fact that in a training set, the true values for

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.040
these nodes in the middle are not observed.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.560
That is, you don't see what they should be in a training set.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:18.760
You see what the inputs are, you see what the outputs should be, but the things in

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.520
a hidden layer are not seen in a training set.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:24.720
So that kind of explains the name hidden layer.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.760
Just means you don't see it in a training set.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:28.560
Let's introduce a bit more notation.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:34.400
Whereas previously we were using the vector x to denote the input features, an

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:39.520
alternative notation for the values of the input features will be a super

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.640
square bracket zero.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:47.480
And the term a also stands for activations, and it refers to the values that

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:52.040
different layers of the neural network are passing on to the subsequent layers.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:56.600
So the input layer passes on the value x to the hidden layer, so we're going to

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:01.560
call that called the activations of the input layer a superscript zero.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:06.160
The next layer, the hidden layer, will in turn generate some set of activations,

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:09.880
which I'm going to write as a superscript square bracket one.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:15.240
So in particular, this first unit or this first node will generate the value a

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.080
superscript square bracket one, subscript one.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:23.200
This second node will generate a value now with a subscript two and so on.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:29.080
And so a superscript square bracket one, this is a four dimensional vector,

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:34.240
or if you want in Python, it gives us a four by one matrix or four column vector,

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:35.480
which looks like this.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:41.120
And it's four dimensional because in this case we have four nodes or four units or

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:43.480
four hidden units in this hidden layer.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:47.200
Then finally, the output layer will generate some value a two,

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:48.600
which is just a real number.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:53.120
And so y hat is going to take on the value of a two.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:58.240
So this is analogous to how in logistic regression, we have y hat equals a and

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:02.280
in logistic regression, we only had that one output layer.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:06.000
So we didn't use the superscript square brackets, but with a neural network,

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:09.360
we're now going to use the superscript square bracket to explicitly

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.320
indicate which layer it came from.

00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:15.920
One funny thing about notational conventions in neural networks is that

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:20.920
this network that you're seeing here is called a two layer neural network.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:24.720
And the reason is that when we count layers in neural networks,

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.400
we don't count the input layer.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.840
So the hidden layer is layer one and the output layer is layer two.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:34.440
In a notational convention, we're calling the input layer layer zero.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:37.880
So technically, maybe there are three layers in this neural network,

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:40.240
because there's the input layer, the hidden layer and the output layer.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:44.120
But in conventional usage, if you read research papers and

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:47.680
elsewhere in the course, you see people refer to this particular neural network as

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:52.600
a two layer neural network because we don't count the input layer as an official layer.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:56.280
Finally, something that we'll get to later is that the hidden layer and

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:59.840
the output layers will have parameters associated with it.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.720
So the hidden layer will have associated with it parameters w and b.

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:09.000
And I'm going to write superscript square bracket one to indicate that these

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.000
are parameters associated with layer one with the hidden layer.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:16.280
We'll see later that w will be a four by three matrix and

00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:21.440
b will be a four by one vector in this example, where the first coordinate four

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:25.040
comes in the fact that we have four nodes or four hidden units in the layer.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:28.520
And three comes from the fact that we have three input features.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:31.560
We'll talk later about the dimensions of these matrices, and

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.280
it might make more sense at that time.

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:38.400
But then similarly, the output layer has associated with it also parameters w,

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:42.400
superscript square bracket two and b, superscript square bracket two.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:46.040
And it turns out the dimensions of these are one by four and one by one.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:49.920
And these one by four is because the hidden layer has four hidden units,

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:52.040
the output layer has just one unit.

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.520
But again, we'll go over the dimensions of these matrices and

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:56.640
vectors in a later video.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:00.080
So you've just seen what a two layer neural network looks like.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:03.080
That is a neural network with one hidden layer.

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:08.120
In the next video, let's go deeper into exactly what this neural network is computing.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:10.880
That is how this neural network inputs x and

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:13.920
goes all the way to computing its output y hat.
